This is the gated, final spec@1 for SPEC-ARCH-004. It passed canon-exit-gate after 4 rounds of adversarial review (the earlier rounds caught real defects: unachievable error-message assertions given napi-derive's actual codegen, an off-by-one Duration-overflow bound that would have shipped a live panic, a missing TypeScript-surface annotation requirement, missing wraparound/negative-input error cases, and two Rust `f64::Display` precision errors in the pinned literals).

```json
{
  "id": "SPEC-ARCH-004",
  "purpose": "Eliminate the resilience-domain half of the NAPI numeric-boundary coercion defect class that SPEC-ARCH-003 already eliminated for the rendering domain. Today next_retry_delay and is_retryable_status in src/napi.rs each pick their own out-of-domain policy for a JS number crossing into michi-resilience: jitter_seed is silently clamped into [0.0, 1.0] instead of rejected, base_delay_ms/max_delay_ms/retry_after_ms are validated by an inline per-call loop instead of a type invariant, max_retries and attempt are read as raw u32 (subject to napi-rs's ToUint32 wraparound coercion on the FromNapiValue path — a negative JS number silently wraps to a large positive u32), and an out-of-range status code is silently defaulted to 0 via `u16::try_from(status).unwrap_or(0)` instead of being rejected. This spec closes every one of those four positions by reusing the JsRanged<const MIN,MAX> kernel and JsFloat type already shipped in src/napi/num.rs, and by adding a small number of new resilience-domain types to the same module (JsUnitInterval, JsDelayMillis, and two new generic accessors on JsRanged) so the class becomes unrepresentable at this boundary, matching the pattern SPEC-ARCH-003 established for rendering.",
  "scope": "The four numeric-parameter positions of next_retry_delay (src/napi.rs, currently starting at line 297) and the one numeric-parameter position of is_retryable_status (currently at line 342), plus the new resilience-domain additions to the existing shared kernel module src/napi/num.rs (declared `pub mod num;` by SPEC-ARCH-003; this spec adds to that module, it does not create a new one).

CONVERSION MECHANISM: identical to SPEC-ARCH-003 — every new type reads the incoming JS value as f64 via `f64::from_napi_value` (non-coercing; napi_get_value_int32/uint32's ToInt32/ToUint32 wraparound is never in the path), routes it through the type's `TryFrom<f64>` impl, and a validation failure becomes `napi::Error::new(napi::Status::InvalidArg, <message>)` thrown by napi-derive-generated glue before the wrapped function body runs. Confirmed against napi-derive-backend 5.1.1's actual codegen (the version this workspace's Cargo.lock resolves for napi-derive = "3"): argument conversion is emitted as a bare `<T as FromNapiValue>::from_napi_value(env, arg)?` with no additional wrapping, so the JS-visible thrown message is EXACTLY the string the type's `TryFrom<f64>` impl returns — no parameter name is or can be injected by the generated glue. Every acceptance criterion in this spec that names an expected error message therefore asserts the type-level message text verbatim, not a parameter name, matching the pattern already shipped by JsCount/JsInt/JsRanged/JsFloat in SPEC-ARCH-003, none of whose error messages mention a field name either. Each new type implements TypeName (value_type() -> napi::ValueType::Number), ValidateNapiValue (empty body), FromNapiValue, and ToNapiValue, exactly as JsCount/JsInt/JsRanged/JsFloat already do.

NEW TYPES ADDED TO src/napi/num.rs (none of these existed before this spec; JsRanged and JsFloat themselves are reused, not redefined):
1. `JsUnitInterval(f64)` — TryFrom<f64> accepts only a finite value with 0.0 <= v <= 1.0; rejects NaN, +/-Infinity, and any value outside [0.0, 1.0], including values that are 'close' (e.g. 1.5, -0.1). No clamping. On rejection returns `Err(format!("expected a finite number in [0.0, 1.0], got {v}"))`. Used for jitter_seed and jitter_factor, both currently f64 parameters of next_retry_delay. Both parameters share this one type and therefore produce byte-identical error text when either is out of domain; distinguishing which of the two failed is not part of this spec's contract (see non_goals).
2. `JsDelayMillis(f64)` — TryFrom<f64> accepts only a finite, non-negative value whose seconds-equivalent (`v / 1000.0`) is strictly less than `u64::MAX as f64` (note: `u64::MAX as f64` rounds UP to exactly 2^64, one greater than the true `u64::MAX`; the bound is deliberately `<`, not `<=`, so that `Duration::from_secs_f64` — whose valid range tops out at `u64::MAX` seconds — never panics on a value this type accepted; a `<=` bound would admit `v = 1.8446744073709552e22`, whose `v / 1000.0` lands exactly on `2^64` and overflows `Duration`. This tightens, not loosens, next_retry_delay's current inline loop, which uses `>` against the same rounded-up bound and has the identical off-by-one exposure today). On rejection — whether the value is non-finite, negative, or too large to convert — returns `Err(format!("expected a finite non-negative number convertible to a Duration (v / 1000.0 < u64::MAX), got {v}"))`, one uniform message for all three rejection reasons, matching JsCount's existing pattern of a single message string covering every violated condition of its `TryFrom<f64>`. Provides `as_duration(self) -> std::time::Duration`, computed as `Duration::from_secs_f64(self.0 / 1000.0)`, which is infallible given the TryFrom bound already holds. Used for base_delay_ms, max_delay_ms (both non-Option f64 today), and retry_after_ms (Option<f64> today, becomes Option<JsDelayMillis>).
3. Two new accessor methods on the existing generic `JsRanged<const MIN, MAX>` (added alongside the existing `get_u8`, not replacing it, and not changing JsRanged's stored representation, its TryFrom<f64> impl, or its existing get()/get_u8() behavior — the existing `expected an integer in [{MIN}, {MAX}], got {v}` message is reused unchanged): `get_u16(self) -> u16`, guarded by a `U16_FITS` const assertion (`MIN >= 0 && MAX <= u16::MAX as i64`) mirroring `U8_FITS`; and `get_u32(self) -> u32`, guarded by a `U32_FITS` const assertion (`MIN >= 0 && MAX <= u32::MAX as i64`) mirroring the same pattern. Neither is `const fn`, for the same rustc-1.96 TryFrom-in-const-fn reason documented on `get_u8`.
4. `JsRetryCount = JsRanged<0, 4_294_967_295>` — a resilience-domain type alias (matching michi_resilience::RetryConfig's `max_retries: u32` and next_retry_delay's `attempt: u32` domains exactly), consumed via the new `get_u32()`. Replaces the raw `u32` parameters `max_retries` and `attempt`. Rejection message (inherited from JsRanged, unchanged): `expected an integer in [0, 4294967295], got {v}`.
5. `JsHttpStatus = JsRanged<100, 599>` — a resilience-domain type alias for the conventional HTTP status-code domain, consumed via the new `get_u16()`. Replaces `is_retryable_status`'s raw `u32` parameter `status`. Rejection message (inherited from JsRanged, unchanged): `expected an integer in [100, 599], got {v}`.

CALL SITES CONVERTED (src/napi.rs, current line numbers):
1. `next_retry_delay`'s `max_retries: u32` (line 298) becomes `#[napi(ts_arg_type = "number")] max_retries: JsRetryCount`.
2. `next_retry_delay`'s `base_delay_ms: f64` (line 299) becomes `#[napi(ts_arg_type = "number")] base_delay_ms: JsDelayMillis`.
3. `next_retry_delay`'s `max_delay_ms: f64` (line 300) becomes `#[napi(ts_arg_type = "number")] max_delay_ms: JsDelayMillis`.
4. `next_retry_delay`'s `jitter_factor: f64` (line 301) becomes `#[napi(ts_arg_type = "number")] jitter_factor: JsUnitInterval`.
5. `next_retry_delay`'s `jitter_seed: f64` (line 302) becomes `#[napi(ts_arg_type = "number")] jitter_seed: JsUnitInterval`, deleting the `.clamp(0.0, 1.0)` at line 328.
6. `next_retry_delay`'s `attempt: u32` (line 303) becomes `#[napi(ts_arg_type = "number")] attempt: JsRetryCount`.
7. `next_retry_delay`'s `retry_after_ms: Option<f64>` (line 304) becomes `#[napi(ts_arg_type = "number | undefined | null")] retry_after_ms: Option<JsDelayMillis>` — the nullable-union override is mandatory here, exactly as SPEC-ARCH-003 required for render_kv's Option<JsCount> total_count parameter, because a bare `"number"` override on an Option-wrapped parameter silently drops the nullable union napi-rs would otherwise generate.
8. The entire inline validation block at lines 306-327 (the two `for (name, val) in [...]` loops and the `if let Some(ms) = retry_after_ms` block) is deleted; validation is now performed once, per parameter, inside each type's `TryFrom<f64>` before `next_retry_delay`'s body runs.
9. `is_retryable_status`'s `status: u32` (line 342) becomes `#[napi(ts_arg_type = "number")] status: JsHttpStatus`, deleting the `u16::try_from(status).unwrap_or(0)` at line 343 and the `#[allow(clippy::cast_possible_truncation, clippy::cast_sign_loss)]` attribute at line 341 (the last of the four such attributes in src/napi.rs; SPEC-ARCH-003 already deleted the other three and left this one for this spec, per its own scope note).

RESULTING SIGNATURES: `pub fn next_retry_delay(max_retries: JsRetryCount, base_delay_ms: JsDelayMillis, max_delay_ms: JsDelayMillis, jitter_factor: JsUnitInterval, jitter_seed: JsUnitInterval, attempt: JsRetryCount, retry_after_ms: Option<JsDelayMillis>) -> napi::Result<Option<f64>>` (return type and Result-wrapping unchanged from today: the function's body itself performs no boundary validation anymore, but napi::Result is retained to match next_retry_delay's existing #[napi(catch_unwind)] convention and because a boundary-validation failure is still surfaced as an Err before the body runs). `pub fn is_retryable_status(status: JsHttpStatus) -> bool` — return type is UNCHANGED: `is_retryable_status` already returns plain `bool` today (not `napi::Result<bool>`), and this spec does not alter that; only the parameter type and the body's internal coercion (`u16::try_from(status).unwrap_or(0)` deleted, replaced by `status.get_u16()`) change.

LINT ENFORCEMENT: src/napi.rs already carries the module-level `#![deny(clippy::as_conversions, clippy::cast_possible_truncation, clippy::cast_sign_loss, clippy::cast_possible_wrap, clippy::cast_precision_loss)]` attribute added by SPEC-ARCH-003. This spec deletes the one remaining `#[allow]` of those lints (on is_retryable_status) without weakening the deny, so after this spec lands src/napi.rs carries zero cast-lint `#[allow]` attributes.

EXISTING TEST UPDATES REQUIRED: the num.rs test `boundary_carries_exactly_one_cast_allow_and_no_residual_coercion` (src/napi/num.rs, current line ~389) currently asserts `napi_src.matches("#[allow(clippy::cast_possible_truncation").count() == 1` (expecting exactly the is_retryable_status attribute to remain) and checks a residual-coercion list of `[".max(0)", "usize::try_from", ".clamp(0, 20)"]`, scanning only src/napi.rs (the test reads `include_str!("../napi.rs")`, not src/napi/num.rs). This spec updates that test to assert the count is 0 and extends the residual-coercion list — still scanned only against src/napi.rs — with `"u16::try_from"` and `".clamp(0.0, 1.0)"`, since both expressions are deleted from src/napi.rs by this spec. Any existing Rust unit test in `src/napi.rs mod tests` that constructs `next_retry_delay` or `is_retryable_status` arguments as bare `f64`/`u32` literals will fail to compile the moment the parameter types change (the new types have no infallible constructor) and must be migrated to `JsUnitInterval::try_from(0.2).expect("0.2 is a valid unit interval")`-style construction, following SPEC-ARCH-003's sanctioned test-construction idiom exactly.",
  "non_goals": [
    "The rendering-domain positions already converted by SPEC-ARCH-003 (JsToonValue.int_val/float_val/decimals_val, JsToonOptions.total_count, render_kv's total_count, truncate's max_chars, JsAgentResponse::total_count). Not touched by this spec.",
    "Outbound numeric positions — next_retry_delay's `-> napi::Result<Option<f64>>` return value and the crate-internal `parse_retry_after -> Option<f64>` helper. Values flowing JS-ward carry no coercion hazard; this spec validates only inbound positions.",
